WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Leslie Gauthier
Reviewer: Joanna Pietrulewicz

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.726
You can ask anyone you want,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.434
and they will tell you

00:00:18.458 --> 00:00:23.042
that they are sick and tired
of fighting for justice.

00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:30.518
People of color and members
of the LGBT community are tired

00:00:30.542 --> 00:00:33.518
of carrying the burden of speaking up

00:00:33.542 --> 00:00:35.226
and stepping up

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.476
even when they're being silenced

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.643
and pushed back down.

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.476
And white allies

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.042
and cis allies are tired, too.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.309
Tired of being told they're doing it wrong

00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:51.750
or that it isn't even their place
to show up at all.

00:00:52.542 --> 00:00:56.893
This fatigue is impacting all of us.

00:00:56.917 --> 00:00:58.476
And in fact,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.268
I believe we won't succeed

00:01:01.292 --> 00:01:04.792
until we approach justice in a new way.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.351
I grew up in the middle
of the civil rights movement

00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:12.333
in the segregated South.

00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:15.143
As a five-year-old girl,

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:18.351
I was very interested in ballet.

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:23.000
It seemed to be the five-year-old-girl
thing to do in the 1960s.

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:27.000
My mother took me to a ballet school.

00:01:27.583 --> 00:01:29.809
You know, the kind of school
that had teachers

00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:31.768
that talked about your gifts and talents

00:01:31.792 --> 00:01:34.018
knowing that you'd never be a ballerina.

00:01:34.042 --> 00:01:36.184
(Laughter)

00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:37.559
When we arrived,

00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:42.417
they said nicely that they
"did not accept Negroes."

00:01:43.458 --> 00:01:47.226
We got back in the car as if we were
just leaving a grocery store

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.292
that was out of orange juice.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.851
We said nothing ...

00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:55.417
just drove to the next ballet school.

00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:00.917
They said, "We don't accept Negroes."

00:02:01.625 --> 00:02:04.018
Well, I was confused.

00:02:04.042 --> 00:02:07.458
And I asked my mother
why they didn't want me.

00:02:08.208 --> 00:02:12.768
And she said, "Well, they're just
not smart enough to accept you right now,

00:02:12.792 --> 00:02:14.851
and they don't know
how excellent you are."

00:02:14.875 --> 00:02:16.143
(Cheers)

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:19.750
(Applause and cheers)

00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:24.726
Well, I didn't know what that meant.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.476
(Laughter)

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.268
But I was sure it wasn't good,

00:02:29.292 --> 00:02:32.226
because I could see it
in my mother's eyes.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.518
She was angry,

00:02:35.542 --> 00:02:38.167
and it looked like she was
on the verge of tears.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:42.851
Well, I decided right then and right there

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:45.167
that ballet was dumb.

00:02:45.542 --> 00:02:46.917
(Laughter)

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:51.018
You know, I had lots of experiences
like that along the way,

00:02:51.042 --> 00:02:53.059
but as I got older,

00:02:53.083 --> 00:02:55.601
I started to get angry.

00:02:55.625 --> 00:03:00.101
And not just angry
at the outright racism and injustice.

00:03:00.125 --> 00:03:04.042
I was angry at people
that stood by and didn't say anything.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.893
Like, why didn't the white parents
in that ballet school say

00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:11.934
"Uh, that's wrong.

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.625
Let that little girl dance."

00:03:14.708 --> 00:03:15.976
Or why --

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.309
(Applause)

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:20.643
Why didn't the white patrons
in the segregated restaurants say

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:22.393
"Hey, that's not right.

00:03:22.417 --> 00:03:24.167
Let that family eat."

00:03:24.958 --> 00:03:27.184
Well, it didn't take me long to realize

00:03:27.208 --> 00:03:30.726
that racial injustice
wasn't the only place

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.351
that people in the majority
were staying quiet.

00:03:34.375 --> 00:03:37.518
When I'd sit in church and hear
some homophobic comment

00:03:37.542 --> 00:03:39.601
being disguised as something scriptural,

00:03:39.625 --> 00:03:42.518
I'd say, "I'm sorry,

00:03:42.542 --> 00:03:47.351
why aren't the heterosexual
churchgoers disrupting this nonsense?"

00:03:47.375 --> 00:03:50.018
(Applause)

00:03:50.042 --> 00:03:51.292
Or ...

00:03:52.292 --> 00:03:56.309
in a room filled with boomers and Gen-Xers

00:03:56.333 --> 00:03:59.018
who started degrading
their millennial colleagues

00:03:59.042 --> 00:04:01.934
as being spoiled, lazy and overconfident,

00:04:01.958 --> 00:04:04.934
I'd say, "I'm sorry,

00:04:04.958 --> 00:04:09.208
why isn't someone my age
saying 'stop stereotyping?'"

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:10.934
(Audience) Yes!

00:04:10.958 --> 00:04:14.143
(Applause)

00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:17.893
I was used to standing up
on issues like this,

00:04:17.917 --> 00:04:20.542
but why wasn't everyone else?

00:04:21.625 --> 00:04:23.518
My fifth grade teacher,

00:04:23.542 --> 00:04:25.393
Mrs. McFarland,

00:04:25.417 --> 00:04:30.542
taught me that justice
requires an accomplice.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.309
Not just anyone will do.

00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:38.476
She said we need unlikely allies

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.559
if we want to see real change happen.

00:04:41.583 --> 00:04:46.934
And for those of us
experiencing injustice up front,

00:04:46.958 --> 00:04:50.184
we need to be willing to accept the help,

00:04:50.208 --> 00:04:52.934
because when we don't,

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:55.083
change takes too long.

00:04:56.083 --> 00:04:59.768
I mean, imagine if heterosexual
and gay people had not come together

00:04:59.792 --> 00:05:02.851
under the banner of marriage equality.

00:05:02.875 --> 00:05:05.226
Or what if President Kennedy

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.083
just wasn't interested
in the civil rights movement?

00:05:09.125 --> 00:05:14.809
Most of our major movements
in this country might have been delayed

00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:16.726
or even dead

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.042
if it weren't for the presence
of unlikely allies.

00:05:22.083 --> 00:05:24.351
When the same people speak up

00:05:24.375 --> 00:05:27.601
in the same ways they've always spoken up,

00:05:27.625 --> 00:05:30.434
the most we'll ever get
are the same results

00:05:30.458 --> 00:05:33.042
over and over again.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.601
You know, allies often
stand on the sidelines

00:05:36.625 --> 00:05:39.101
waiting to be called up.

00:05:39.125 --> 00:05:44.018
But what if unlikely allies
led out in front of issues?

00:05:44.042 --> 00:05:45.393
Like ...

00:05:45.417 --> 00:05:49.417
what if Black and Native American people
stood in front of immigration issues?

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.917
(Applause)

00:05:54.208 --> 00:05:58.643
Or what if white people led the charge

00:05:58.667 --> 00:06:00.684
to end racism?

00:06:00.708 --> 00:06:04.708
(Applause and cheers)

00:06:05.625 --> 00:06:06.976
Or ...

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.476
what if men led the charge
on pay equity for women?

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:15.476
(Applause and cheers)

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:16.934
Or ...

00:06:16.958 --> 00:06:22.726
what if heterosexual people
stood in front of LGBTQ issues?

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:26.851
(Applause and cheers)

00:06:26.875 --> 00:06:30.643
And what if able-bodied people advocated

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:33.226
for people living with disabilities?

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:37.101
(Applause and cheers)

00:06:37.125 --> 00:06:39.768
You know, we can stand up for issues,

00:06:39.792 --> 00:06:42.101
weigh in and advocate

00:06:42.125 --> 00:06:46.375
even when it seems like the issue
has nothing to do with us.

00:06:47.125 --> 00:06:48.434
And actually,

00:06:48.458 --> 00:06:51.684
those are the issues
that are most compelling.

00:06:51.708 --> 00:06:53.208
And sure,

00:06:53.917 --> 00:06:58.018
people will have no idea
why you are there,

00:06:58.042 --> 00:07:01.351
but that's why those of us
facing injustice

00:07:01.375 --> 00:07:04.268
must be willing to accept the help.

00:07:04.292 --> 00:07:07.226
You know, we have to fight injustice

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:09.542
with a consciousness of grace.

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:13.226
When white guys stand up to fight

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.518
for the liberation
of Black and Brown people,

00:07:16.542 --> 00:07:20.542
Black and Brown people will have to
be willing to accept their help.

00:07:21.708 --> 00:07:24.226
And I know that's complicated,

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:27.684
but this is collective work

00:07:27.708 --> 00:07:32.958
and it requires everyone to be all in.

00:07:34.208 --> 00:07:36.809
One day when I was at kindergarten,

00:07:36.833 --> 00:07:38.309
our teacher introduced us

00:07:38.333 --> 00:07:41.875
to this beautiful, tall,
white lady named Miss Ann.

00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:45.833
I thought she was the prettiest
white lady I'd ever seen.

00:07:46.667 --> 00:07:48.309
Well, if I can be honest with you,

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:51.976
I think it was the first time we'd ever
seen a white lady in our school ever.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.268
(Laughter)

00:07:53.292 --> 00:07:54.851
Miss Ann stood in front of us,

00:07:54.875 --> 00:07:59.143
and she said she was going
to start teaching ballet classes

00:07:59.167 --> 00:08:01.042
right there are our school

00:08:02.042 --> 00:08:05.875
and that she was proud
to be our dance teacher.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.375
It was unreal.

00:08:10.375 --> 00:08:12.226
All of a sudden --

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:15.309
(sings) I didn't think
ballet was dumb anymore.

00:08:15.333 --> 00:08:17.083
(Laughter)

00:08:17.875 --> 00:08:22.934
You see, what I know now
is Miss Ann was fully aware

00:08:22.958 --> 00:08:27.768
that the white ballet schools
would not accept Black girls.

00:08:27.792 --> 00:08:30.268
She was incensed by that.

00:08:30.292 --> 00:08:32.809
So she came to the Black neighborhood

00:08:32.833 --> 00:08:36.000
to start teaching
the dance classes herself.

00:08:36.958 --> 00:08:40.059
And you know, it took love
and courage for her to do that.

00:08:40.083 --> 00:08:43.083
(Applause)

00:08:46.667 --> 00:08:50.143
And where there was no justice,

00:08:50.167 --> 00:08:51.792
she just built it.

00:08:53.167 --> 00:08:56.143
We all survived,

00:08:56.167 --> 00:09:00.000
because we stood on the shoulders
of our Black ancestors.

00:09:00.792 --> 00:09:06.625
We all thrived, because
Miss Ann was an unlikely ally.

00:09:07.667 --> 00:09:10.768
You know, when you add your voice

00:09:10.792 --> 00:09:12.934
and your actions

00:09:12.958 --> 00:09:16.726
to situations that you
don't think involve you,

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:19.625
you actually inspire others
to do the same.

00:09:20.625 --> 00:09:24.726
Miss Ann inspired me
to always be on the lookout

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:28.559
for situations that weren't about me

00:09:28.583 --> 00:09:30.559
but where I saw injustice

00:09:30.583 --> 00:09:33.333
and inequality happening anyway.

00:09:34.375 --> 00:09:36.976
I hope she inspires you, too,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.268
because to win the fight for equity

00:09:42.292 --> 00:09:46.101
we will all need to speak up

00:09:46.125 --> 00:09:47.625
and stand up.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.934
We will all need to do that.

00:09:50.958 --> 00:09:52.976
And we will all need to do that

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.851
even when it's hard

00:09:54.875 --> 00:09:58.018
and even when we feel out of place,

00:09:58.042 --> 00:10:02.018
because it is your place,

00:10:02.042 --> 00:10:04.375
and it is our place.

00:10:05.125 --> 00:10:09.226
Justice counts on all of us.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:10.559
Thank you.

00:10:10.583 --> 00:10:13.417
(Applause and cheers)

